import pf.Html
import ../../../Demos/PushCompose
import ../../../Demos/PushStates
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Demo
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Note
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths

## The push package, a plugin: the package's example app file by file, `<PushButton>` and its
## states, a notification and its options (live: the package's functions in the browser), sending,
## how Web Push goes, the modules, safety, and what works today and what waits on a proposal.
## Static but for the notification demo: this site is files, and a push needs a server.
meta = {
	title: "Push",
	description: "Web Push notifications for Roc Solid apps, as a plugin: a button that subscribes, a table that keeps each browser's subscription, and jobs that send. Sending waits on the server's host.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "example", label: "A first app" },
		{ id: "button", label: "<PushButton>" },
		{ id: "notification", label: "A notification" },
		{ id: "sending", label: "Sending" },
		{ id: "how", label: "How it works" },
		{ id: "modules", label: "The modules" },
		{ id: "safety", label: "Safety" },
		{ id: "status", label: "Status" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/push</code>]}
				title="Notifications that arrive with every tab closed"
			>
				Web Push lets a server send a notification to a browser that agreed to receive them, even when none
				of the site's tabs is open. This package is a plugin with every part of it: a button that asks and
				subscribes, a table that keeps each browser's subscription, and a job that sends.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				Use it for news a user wants even when they aren't looking at your site: a reply to their question,
				an export that finished, an order that shipped. One piece is still missing. The server's host can't
				yet do the encryption Web Push needs, so today every send fails and says why (see
				<a href="#status">Status</a>). Everything else works and is tested.
			</p>

			<Heading id="example">A first app</Heading>
			<p>
				The package has a small app, <code>packages/push/example</code>: a page with the subscribe button and
				a button that notifies everyone. Each file below is that app's. This site can't run it, because the
				site is static files and Web Push needs a server.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>1. Keys.</strong> Web Push identifies your server by a key pair, called the VAPID keys.
				<code>node packages/push/vapid-keys.mjs</code> prints one. Give the server
				<code>VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY</code>, <code>VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY</code> and <code>VAPID_SUBJECT</code>
				(<code>mailto:</code> and an address, or an <code>https:</code> URL) in its environment. The private
				key never leaves the server. A browser that subscribed with one public key can only be sent to with
				that pair, so keep the keys once you have users. The plugin needs all three: if one is missing, or
				the two keys don't belong together, the server doesn't start, and says why.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>2. The plugin.</strong> Name the package in the app's header and list its plugin, together
				with the <a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>jobs</a> plugin, which runs the job that sends:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.push_example_main.file} source={Sources.push_example_main.rocx} />
			<p>That is all the server needs. The plugin brings:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					three endpoints, behind the app's middleware: <code>GET /push/key</code> (the public key),
					<code>POST /push/subscribe</code> and <code>POST /push/unsubscribe</code>;
				</li>
				<li>its table, <code>push_subscriptions</code>, in the app's database (Postgres or SQLite);</li>
				<li>its job, <code>push.deliver</code>, which the jobs plugin's workers run;</li>
				<li>
					the service worker at <code>/push-sw.js</code>, and a script every page loads. Both are served before
					the app's middleware, so a guard never blocks them.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>Two options change the defaults:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<code>{"Push.plugin({ at: \"/notifications\" })"}</code> puts the endpoints under another path (give
					the button the same path).
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>{"Push.plugin({ owner: |context| context.member })"}</code> makes each subscription belong to the
					signed-in user, read from the context your middleware hands on. Then <code>Push.notify!</code> can
					send to that user's browsers. Without it, every subscription is a visitor's (<code>""</code>).
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<strong>3. The page.</strong> The button, and a handler that asks an endpoint to notify everyone:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.push_example_page.file} source={Sources.push_example_page.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.push_example_announce.file} source={Sources.push_example_announce.rocx} />

			<Heading id="button">{"<PushButton>"}</Heading>
			<p>
				A button that turns this browser's notifications on, and off again. A click calls the plugin's script
				(<code>static/push.js</code>) with <a href={"${Paths.components}#handlers"}><code>Browser.call!</code></a>.
				The script asks the browser's permission, registers the service worker, subscribes with the server's
				public key, and posts the subscription to the server. Turning notifications off unsubscribes and tells
				the server. Browsers only show the permission prompt during a click, a key press or a tap, which is why
				subscribing starts from a button.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Prop</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><code>api : Str</code></td>
							<td>
								where the plugin's endpoints are: with <code>"/push"</code>, <code>GET /push/key</code>,
								<code>POST /push/subscribe</code> and <code>POST /push/unsubscribe</code>
							</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>Browser.call!</code> can't return an answer, so the script reports how a click went with a
				<code>pushstate</code> event, which every <code>{"<PushButton>"}</code> on the page hears. When the page
				loads, the script also reports where this browser stands. The button turns each report into a
				<code>PushState</code> and shows it:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>The script says</th><th>The button shows</th></tr></thead>
					<PushStates />
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>aria-pressed</code> on the button says whether notifications are on, and a screen reader reads
				the note when it changes (<code>aria-live</code>). On an iPhone or an iPad, a site gets Web Push only
				once it is added to the Home Screen and opened from there.
			</p>
			<details>
				<summary>The button: <code>{Sources.push_button.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.push_button.file} source={Sources.push_button.rocx} />
			</details>

			<Heading id="notification">A notification</Heading>
			<p>
				A notification is a title, a body, the page a click opens (<code>url</code>) and a <code>tag</code>.
				Its options tell the push service how to treat the message. Change them below: the package's own
				functions, running in your browser, show what a server would send.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo>
			<div data-demo="push-compose"><PushCompose /></div>
		</Demo>
		<NoHydration>
			<Note>
				The request has two more headers: <code>Authorization</code> (the VAPID token, <code>vapid t=…, k=…</code>)
				and <code>Content-Encoding: aes128gcm</code>. The data travels encrypted: the push service sees
				only bytes.
			</Note>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>title : Str</code></td><td>the notification's first line; empty: the site's host name</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>body : Str</code></td><td>the text under it</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>url : Str</code></td><td>the page a click opens, on this site only; empty: the front page</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>tag : Str</code></td><td>a newer notification with the same tag replaces this one, rather than piling up</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>icon : Str</code></td><td>an image's URL</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>{"Notification.new(title, body)"}</code> makes one with the other fields empty, and
				<code>{"{ ..Notification.new(title, body), url: \"/orders/17\" }"}</code> sets more. Empty fields are
				left out of the JSON.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><code>ttl : U64</code></td>
							<td>
								how many seconds the push service keeps the message for a browser that is offline. 0 means now
								or never; more than four weeks is sent as four weeks.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>urgency : [VeryLow, Low, Normal, High]</code></td>
							<td>how soon it must arrive. A phone on battery may hold the low ones back.</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>topic : Str</code></td>
							<td>
								a newer message with the same topic replaces one still waiting, so only the latest status
								arrives. At most 32 characters, of letters, digits, <code>-</code> and <code>_</code>.
							</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p><code>Options.defaults</code> is a day, <code>Normal</code>, and no topic.</p>
			<details>
				<summary>This demo: <code>{Sources.push_compose.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.push_compose.file} source={Sources.push_compose.rocx} />
			</details>

			<Heading id="sending">Sending</Heading>
			<p>
				On the server, a job or an endpoint calls <code>Push.notify!</code> (one user's browsers) or
				<code>Push.broadcast!</code> (every browser). Each checks the notification and its options, then
				queues a <code>push.deliver</code> job for each subscription and answers how many it queued. Inside a
				transaction, the jobs are queued only if the transaction commits.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.push_send.file} source={Sources.push_send.rocx} />
			<p>A jobs worker sends each message. The push service's answer decides what happens next:</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>The push service answers</th><th>The job</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td>201, or any 2xx</td><td>is done, and notes when it sent</td></tr>
						<tr><td>404 or 410</td><td>deletes the subscription: the browser unsubscribed, or it expired</td></tr>
						<tr><td>408, 429 or 5xx, with <code>Retry-After</code></td><td>sends it again at that time, five times at most, while the message's TTL lasts</td></tr>
						<tr><td>408, 429 or 5xx without it, or no answer</td><td>fails the attempt; the worker tries again, waiting longer each time (five attempts)</td></tr>
						<tr><td>400, 401, 403, 413, or anything else</td><td>logs it and notes it on the subscription; sending again wouldn't help</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				A retry sends the TTL that is left, so a message never outlives its TTL. A 403 doesn't delete the
				subscription: push services answer 403 when the VAPID keys are wrong, and a mistake in the server's
				settings shouldn't delete every subscription.
			</p>

			<Heading id="how">How it works</Heading>
			<ol>
				<li>
					A click on <code>{"<PushButton>"}</code> asks the browser's permission.
				</li>
				<li>
					The browser subscribes at its push service (Google's for Chrome, Mozilla's for Firefox, Apple's
					for Safari, Microsoft's for Edge) with the server's public key. It gets a subscription: a URL at
					that service, and two keys.
				</li>
				<li>The page posts the subscription to the app, which keeps it in a table, a row for each browser.</li>
				<li>Later, the server calls <code>Push.notify!</code>, which queues a job for each of the user's subscriptions.</li>
				<li>
					The job encrypts the notification for that one browser (RFC 8291), signs a token that says which
					server sent it (VAPID, RFC 8292), and posts both to the subscription's URL (RFC 8030).
				</li>
				<li>
					The push service wakes the browser. The package's service worker shows the notification, and a
					click on it opens the page it names.
				</li>
			</ol>
			<p>The package has three parts:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Plain Roc</strong>, on no platform: the formats, the key derivation, the token, and what
					each answer means. <code>roc test packages/push/main.roc</code> checks them against the RFCs' own
					examples. The browser's half of an app can use these too (<code>browser.roc</code> lists them).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The plugin</strong>, <code>{"Push.plugin({})"}</code>, on the server platform, which the
					package names in its header (<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>plugins</a>): its endpoints,
					its table, and the job that sends.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The browser's part</strong>: <code>{"<PushButton>"}</code> (in <code>components/</code>,
					which <code>rocx build</code> copies into the app), and the page's script and the service worker
					(in <code>static/</code>), which the plugin serves. The worker is served at the site's root, so its
					scope is the whole site; the script is loaded by every page. When the push service replaces a
					subscription, the worker tells the server, and the script sends the server the browser's
					subscription again once a visit, in case it lost it.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="modules">The modules</Heading>
			<p>
				<strong>Plain Roc</strong>, on no platform, which both halves of an app can use. The full list of
				functions is in <code>packages/push/README.md</code>.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Subscription</code></td><td>a browser's subscription: <code>parse</code> (the page's JSON), <code>from_body</code> (an endpoint's body), <code>from_text</code> and <code>to_text</code> (a table row), <code>to_json</code>; <code>known_service</code>: is it at Chrome's, Firefox's, Safari's or Edge's push service?</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Notification</code></td><td>what the service worker shows: <code>new</code>, <code>to_json</code>, <code>data</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Options</code></td><td>how a push service treats a message: <code>defaults</code>, <code>check</code> (the topic), <code>headers</code>, <code>remaining</code> (the TTL left for a retry)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Delivery</code></td><td><code>request</code> (the POST to a subscription's URL); <code>outcome</code> (what an answer means: <code>Delivered</code>, <code>Gone</code>, <code>RetryAfter(ms)</code>, <code>Retry(why)</code>, <code>Refused(why)</code>), <code>retry_after</code>, <code>http_date</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Vapid</code></td><td>the server's token, RFC 8292: <code>Keys</code> and <code>from_text</code> (from the environment), <code>audience</code>, <code>claims</code> (valid 12 hours), <code>signing_input</code>, <code>token</code>, <code>authorization</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>WebPush</code></td><td>the message's encryption, RFC 8291: <code>prepare</code> (everything before the AES), <code>body</code>, <code>max_data</code> (3993 bytes)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Ece</code></td><td>the <code>aes128gcm</code> content coding, RFC 8188: <code>header</code>, <code>parse_header</code>, <code>pad</code>, <code>unpad</code>, <code>keys</code>, <code>nonce</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>PushState</code></td><td>where a browser stands, as the page's script reports it: what <code>{"<PushButton>"}</code> shows</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				HMAC-SHA-256, HKDF-SHA-256 and base64url come from <a href={Paths.ecosystem_crypto}>the crypto
				package</a>, which push names in its own header: an app names <code>push</code> alone.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>The server's part</strong>, on the server platform, with the table's migrations
				(<code>migrations/</code>, one for Postgres and one for SQLite). Each function's <code>owner</code> is
				the app's id for a user, as text, or <code>""</code> for a visitor.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Push</code></td><td><code>plugin(setup)</code>, the plugin (<code>at</code>, <code>owner</code>); <code>subscribe!(body, owner)</code> and <code>unsubscribe!(endpoint, owner)</code>, which its endpoints call; <code>notify!</code> and <code>broadcast!</code>; <code>public_key!</code> and <code>keys!</code> (from the environment); <code>deliver</code>, the job; <code>send!</code>, one attempt</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>PushStore</code></td><td>the <code>push_subscriptions</code> table: <code>save!</code> (a row for each endpoint), <code>remove!</code>, <code>remove_id!</code>, <code>find!</code>, <code>of_owner!</code>, <code>everyone!</code>, and <code>delivered!</code> and <code>refused!</code>, which the job calls</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p><strong>The browser's files</strong>, in <code>static/</code>, which the plugin serves:</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>File</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>push.js</code></td><td>the page's script, at <code>/rocx/plugins/push/push.js</code>, on every page: the <code>push.subscribe</code> and <code>push.unsubscribe</code> effects the button calls. Once a visit, it sends the server the browser's subscription again. On a site served under a base path (<code>/rocx/</code>), every URL it uses is under that path.</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>push-sw.js</code></td><td>the service worker, at the site's root (<code>/push-sw.js</code>). It shows a message's notification; a click focuses a tab already at its URL, or opens one. When the push service replaces a subscription, it tells the server.</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<Heading id="safety">Safety</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					The server sends a request to whatever URL a subscription names. So <code>Push.subscribe!</code>
					accepts only <code>https:</code> URLs at the browsers' own push services. When the server runs with
					<code>--dev</code> (as <code>rocx dev</code> starts it), it also accepts an <code>http:</code> URL on
					this machine, for a stand-in push service.
				</li>
				<li>The private key never leaves the server. The page gets only the public key.</li>
				<li>A click on a notification opens only this site's pages.</li>
				<li>Messages are encrypted for the browser: the push service sees only bytes.</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="status">Status</Heading>
			<p>What works today:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					Everything that is plain Roc, checked by <code>expect</code>s against the RFCs' own examples: the
					keys, the VAPID token's claims and signing input, the content coding, the message's key derivation
					(up to the AES), the request's headers, and what each answer means.
				</li>
				<li>
					The plugin, built into the example app and run on SQLite, and on Postgres when one is available. It
					saves and forgets subscriptions and queues a job for each; each job reads its subscription and then
					fails, saying the host can't encrypt yet.
				</li>
				<li>
					The button, the page's script and the service worker, tried in Chromium behind a guard that refuses
					every script the app would serve: the plugin's files come through all the same, the worker's scope is
					the whole site, and the button subscribes.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>Not yet (each has a proposal in <code>packages/push/PROPOSALS.md</code>):</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Sending.</strong> The server's host has no P-256, which Web Push needs for the key agreement
					that encrypts a message and for the signature on the VAPID token. It also needs AES-128-GCM with a
					nonce it derives, which the host doesn't offer. Until the host has both, every delivery fails and says
					so. The proposal sketches them on <code>ring</code>, which the host already links, and the sketch
					passes the RFCs' examples.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Jobs that give up, or come back at a set time.</strong> Today a refused message ends its job
					as if it had been sent, and a <code>Retry-After</code> queues a copy of the job for that time.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>Browser.call!</code> with an answer.</strong> Until a call can answer, the script tells
					the button how it went with a DOM event (<code>pushstate</code>).
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				And a limit: a message holds 3993 bytes of data, the notification's JSON. <code>Push.notify!</code>
				refuses a longer one.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
